Subject: Shipping plan balancing cost and carbon impact under real-world operating rules
Date: [Insert date]

Context
Our network ships product from multiple warehouses to a diverse set of stores. Each available route is evaluated on both monetary cost per unit and carbon emissions per unit. In practice, the lowest-cost lanes frequently carry higher emissions, while lower-emission alternatives can raise transportation spend. We need a plan that balances budget discipline with our sustainability commitments without compromising service.

Operational rules we must respect
- Route activation costs: Launching any lane requires a fixed setup outlay that is incurred once upon activation (e.g., contract onboarding, customs documentation, dock preparation). A route is either active or inactive; if active, the setup cost applies regardless of volume moved.
- Minimum order quantities: To justify carrier capacity and handling overhead, any activated lane must move at or above a specified minimum volume during the period. Partial loads below this threshold are not accepted.
- Supplier limits per store: To manage receiving dock constraints, scheduling, and vendor complexity, each store may receive from no more than a limited number of distinct warehouses in the planning window.

Supply and demand boundaries
- Warehouses can only ship up to their available inventory for the period.
- Each store has a required demand that must be met to protect on-shelf availability and service levels.

Management priority: blending cost and sustainability
Leadership has set relative importance weights for transportation spend and emissions. We will evaluate plans using a blended score: the variable shipping costs weighted by the cost importance, plus the emissions weighted by the emissions importance, with route setup costs added in full. This approach makes the trade-off transparent, enabling rapid scenario testing as priorities evolve.

Goal
Identify which shipping routes to activate and how much to move on each active route so that:
- We do not exceed warehouse inventories and we meet each store’s demand.
- Every activated lane ships at least its minimum order quantity.
- Each store is served by no more than the allowed number of warehouses.
- The resulting blended score—combining weighted variable costs, weighted emissions, and full setup costs—is as low as possible while maintaining operational feasibility.

Proposed approach
- Use the latest cost and emissions data by lane to quantify trade-offs under the current management weights.
- Enforce all practical rules (activation costs, minimum volumes, and supplier limits) alongside supply and demand requirements.
- Produce a recommended shipping plan and a small set of alternatives highlighting the budget and carbon implications of shifting weights or relaxing/strengthening operational limits.

Annex – Data Inputs (template placeholders)
- Number of warehouses (sources): {NUM_SOURCES}
- Number of stores (destinations): {NUM_DESTINATIONS}
- Warehouse supply capacities: {SUPPLIES}
- Store demand requirements: {DEMANDS}
- Variable shipping cost per unit by route: {COST_MATRIX}
- Emissions per unit by route (kg carbon dioxide): {EMISSION_MATRIX}
- Cost priority weight: {WEIGHT_COST}
- Emissions priority weight: {WEIGHT_EMISSION}
- Route activation setup costs: {FIXED_COSTS}
- Minimum order quantities per activated route: {MIN_ORDER_QUANTITIES}
- Maximum number of supplying warehouses per store: {MAX_SUPPLIERS_PER_STORE}
